---
import { num, pct, month, pageUrl } from "../i18n";
const { track } = Astro.props;
const lang = "en";
---

<section class="stack prose" aria-labelledby="how">
  <h2 id="how">How the estimate is built</h2>
  <p>
    INSEE publishes the industrial production index about 40 days after each month ends. In the meantime, other data
    that come out faster already say something about activity. This estimate combines them to put a number on the
    month before the official release.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Electricity.</strong> National consumption published by RTE, corrected for temperature, day of the week
      and month of the year. Without that correction, a cold month would look like an industrial boom.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Business climate.</strong> INSEE’s summary indicator from its business surveys.</li>
    <li><strong>Insolvencies.</strong> Insolvency proceedings opened, from BODACC notices.</li>
    <li><strong>Interest rates.</strong> The gap between French and German 10-year yields, published by the ECB.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A regularised linear model (ridge regression) links these signals to the monthly change in production. Each data
    point is used only from its actual publication date, so an estimate never sees data that wasn’t yet known.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is tested</h2>
  <p>
    For every month since {month(lang, track.from)}, the model is retrained only on the data available at that date,
    then compared with the figure INSEE published later. It competes with a naive forecast that carries forward the
    latest known change. March to July 2020 are left out of training: their extreme swings distorted what the model
    learned.
  </p>
  <p>
    Result, excluding the pandemic: a root mean squared error of {num(lang, track.rmse, 2)} points
    against {num(lang, track.rmse_ar, 2)} for the naive forecast, {pct(lang, (1 - track.ratio) * 100, 0, false)} better. The
    Diebold–Mariano test gives p = {num(lang, track.dm_p, 2)}: an encouraging gain, but not statistically proven at
    the 5% level.
  </p>

  <h2>Known limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Revised data.</strong> INSEE revises past figures, and the test uses the latest version, so the scores
      below are somewhat optimistic. Since October 2026 every release is archived as published, to measure real-time
      performance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>September.</strong> Since 2022, weather-corrected electricity drops sharply every September, probably
      because habits changed after the energy crisis. The September estimate is likely biased downward. The variants tested to correct it, and why they weren’t
      adopted, are on the <a href={pageUrl("model", lang)}>Model page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The month before.</strong> The same model also estimates the previous month, without using the extra
      month of production published in between.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sources and licences</h2>
  <p>
    RTE éCO2mix via ODRE, INSEE and BODACC (DILA): Licence Ouverte / Etalab 2.0. Interest rates: European Central Bank.
    Temperatures: Open-Meteo, CC BY 4.0. The code and the full data history are public on GitHub: the code under the MIT licence, the data produced by
    this project under CC BY 4.0.
  </p>
</section>

